From 600898d20e32cbd6962ac59a5901dfab03cd90ea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 00:29:02 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] [DOSKUTSU] patch 0298 -- per-slot drum envelopes on OPL3 and
 OPL2

PATCH_DRUM was one maximally-percussive noise burst -- MULT=15, AR=15,
DR=15, RR=15, WS=7 -- applied to every drum in the kit. The whole kit
therefore differed only by the MIDI note's pitch, so a kick and a closed
hat were the same sound an octave apart. The Shack is the audible case.

The reachable key set is fixed and knowable, not open-ended. The MIDI set
is our own org2mid output and its DRUM_GM_KEY[] table maps the eight
Organya drum SLOTS to exactly eight GM keys: 35, 38, 42, 46, 45, 39, plus
37 and 49 as forward-compat stubs for slots 6/7, which are SND_NULL
across all 42 stock .org files. The engine agrees on the same axis --
Organya.cpp indexes DrumSamples[idx - 8], so the drum sound is chosen by
slot, and slot is what org2mid keys on. Eight envelopes therefore cover
the corpus exactly, and the default case is unreachable with stock data.
It is kept anyway so a hand-authored MIDI on channel 9 degrades to the
old single burst rather than to silence.

Character per key, and the FM parameter doing the work:

  35 KICK       low MULT + sine WS -> pitched body rather than noise
  38 SNARE      high MULT + logsaw -> noisy, decay shorter than the tom
  42 HAT CLOSED max MULT, fastest DR/RR -> shortest event in the kit
  46 HAT OPEN   42's timbre with DR/RR relaxed, so it reads as the SAME
                instrument ringing on rather than a different one
  45 TOM        low MULT + sine -> pitched like the kick, longer decay
  39 PERC       noisy like the snare, slower attack for a softer edge
  37 SIDESTICK  stub: short, dry, pitched
  49 CRASH      stub: noisy, longest RR in the table

Both backends or neither: OPL2 has its own drum path and AdLib is the
configuration where the Shack was completely silent, so a fix in the OPL3
file alone would have left the worse case untouched. The OPL2 table is a
byte-identical copy rather than a shared symbol, matching how that file
already carries its own copies of all eight PATCH_* constants; only one
backend is constructed per process (adlib XOR opl3).

`note` is threaded into _apply_patch on both backends. On the drum channel
GM puts the kit on the note and the program field is ignored, so the note
IS the instrument selector there -- without it the function cannot choose.
The transpose lookup that reads patch[11] is switched to the same table in
the same commit; leaving it on PATCH_DRUM would have applied one envelope's
fine-tune to another's voice.

Rhythm mode was checked before any of this was written, because per-slot
envelopes allocated as ordinary melodic voices would interact with the
OPL3 percussion latch if it were engaged: the failure mode is some drums
working and others not, which reads as a bad envelope rather than a
channel-allocation problem. Every 0xBD write in the tree is a clear to
0x00 -- OPL3 init, OPL2 init, and both shutdowns. Bit 5 is never set
anywhere, so rhythm mode is off, channels 6-8 stay melodic, and there is
no interaction. Negative result, established first rather than after.

NOT VERIFIED BY EAR. These envelopes are authored from the OPL3 register
semantics documented at the head of MidiBackendOpl3.cpp, not tuned against
the chip, and nobody has heard them. Round F's EAR cell is the gate. They
are a first pass that should be expected to need revision -- revise the
table, which is data, rather than reverting the mechanism, which is the
note-threading. A wrong envelope is a wrong constant; the old behaviour
was a wrong design.

Chip note carried in the OPL2 file: bytes [4]/[10] carry WS=7, an OPL3
waveform. A real OPL2 honours only WS 0-3 and masks the rest, so the noisy
entries land on quarter-sine instead of logsaw. That is pre-existing on
this path -- the PATCH_DRUM it replaces already shipped WS=7 -- and is left
alone deliberately: changing it is a timbre decision wanting the operator's
ear, not a silent divergence introduced under cover of an unheard patch.

No banner: this is a data table plus an argument, not a lever with a boot
witness. No killswitch either -- the old behaviour is a single envelope
applied eight ways, which is not a configuration worth preserving.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Code <noreply@anthropic.com>
